00:45:06,880 --> 00:45:09,320
and I practice using it every Tuesday.

1083
00:45:09,320 --> 00:45:10,520
That's hard evidence.

1084
00:45:10,520 --> 00:45:11,480
Okay.

1085
00:45:11,480 --> 00:45:15,160
But choosing a manageable soft skill like delegation

1086
00:45:15,160 --> 00:45:16,480
can often be better

1087
00:45:16,480 --> 00:45:18,280
as long as it doesn't totally sabotage

1088
00:45:18,280 --> 00:45:20,080
the main duties of the role.

1089
00:45:20,080 --> 00:45:23,200
So MacGuff's advice is to pick a weakness

1090
00:45:23,200 --> 00:45:24,800
that is real for you,

1091
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:26,840
but that doesn't directly stop you

1092
00:45:26,840 --> 00:45:28,600
from doing the job you're applying for.

1093
00:45:28,600 --> 00:45:29,440
Right.

1094
00:45:29,440 --> 00:45:31,080
If you're applying to be a data analyst,

1095
00:45:31,080 --> 00:45:33,360
You don't say, well, I struggle with basic math.

1096
00:45:33,360 --> 00:45:34,360
That's a red flag.

1097
00:45:34,360 --> 00:45:35,720
It's a huge red flag.

1098
00:45:35,720 --> 00:45:37,960
A great example she uses is delegation skills.

1099
00:45:37,960 --> 00:45:38,800
Okay.

1100
00:45:38,800 --> 00:45:39,920
If you're applying for a job

1101
00:45:39,920 --> 00:45:43,280
where you're an individual contributor, not a manager,

1102
00:45:43,280 --> 00:45:46,320
being a slightly ineffective delegator isn't a deal breaker.

1103
00:45:46,320 --> 00:45:48,360
And it's a real struggle for a lot of high achievers.

1104
00:45:48,360 --> 00:45:49,440
So that's your 10%.

1105
00:45:49,440 --> 00:45:50,520
That's your 10%.

1106
00:45:50,520 --> 00:45:52,600
Then you maximize the 90%

1107
00:45:52,600 --> 00:45:55,760
to show that tangible behavioral proof of your growth.

1108
00:45:55,760 --> 00:45:56,920
You don't just say, I'm working on it.

1109
00:45:56,920 --> 00:45:58,080
You give them the details.

1110
00:45:58,080 --> 00:45:59,960
You detail the steps.

1111
00:45:59,960 --> 00:46:04,220
I realized I had a bit of a control issue so I implemented a system.

1112
00:46:04,220 --> 00:46:10,140
Every Monday I review my tasks and consciously assign at least two decapable team members.

1113
00:46:10,140 --> 00:46:13,060
I even keep a sticky note on my monitor to remind me.

1114
00:46:13,060 --> 00:46:15,640
That tangible evidence is so powerful.

1115
00:46:15,640 --> 00:46:19,980
As someone who's hired a lot of people, when I hear that I don't see a flaw.

1116
00:46:19,980 --> 00:46:20,980
What do you see?

1117
00:46:20,980 --> 00:46:25,560
I see self-awareness, critical thinking, and a system for self-management.

1118
00:46:25,560 --> 00:46:29,280
It tells me exactly how I can best support them as their manager.

1119
00:46:29,280 --> 00:46:30,620
And that's the goal.

1120
00:46:30,620 --> 00:46:35,000
The desired result, as McGoff says, is you want the interviewer to have an empathetic

1121
00:46:35,000 --> 00:46:36,000
thought.

1122
00:46:36,000 --> 00:46:37,000
You want them to think.

1123
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:38,000
Oh yeah.

1124
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:39,000
I struggle with that too.

1125
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:41,720
This is a real person who takes charge of their own development.

1126
00:46:41,720 --> 00:46:44,520
If you get that response, you've passed the test.

1127
00:46:44,520 --> 00:46:47,600
So we've covered the internal mindset and the big interview question.

1128
00:46:47,600 --> 00:46:50,760
Let's move to the really immediate tactical stuff.

1129
00:46:50,760 --> 00:46:51,760
The toolkit.

1130
00:46:51,760 --> 00:46:52,760
The toolkit.

1131
00:46:52,760 --> 00:46:56,160
What are the specific words we can swap out right now to sound more competent

1132
00:46:56,160 --> 00:46:58,840
and confident in our professional language?

1133
00:46:58,840 --> 00:47:02,460
of English isn't your first language and you're feeling a bit nervous.

1134
00:47:02,460 --> 00:47:07,500
This is where the power of simple declaration is just huge.

1135
00:47:07,500 --> 00:47:13,100
MacGuff's first hack is replacing all that hesitant language, the hedges, the disclaimers.

1136
00:47:13,100 --> 00:47:14,500
I think, I believe.

1137
00:47:14,500 --> 00:47:15,500
Yes.

1138
00:47:15,500 --> 00:47:20,270
Replace all of that with one single declarative word, confident.

1139
00:47:20,270 --> 00:47:23,870
It's the difference between sounding like you're asking for permission to be good

1140
00:47:23,870 --> 00:47:27,310
at your job and sounding like you expect to be good at your job.

1141
00:47:27,310 --> 00:47:28,310
Exactly.

1142
00:47:28,310 --> 00:47:32,950
time. I think that I'd be good at this role or I believe I have the skills for this.

1143
00:47:32,950 --> 00:47:35,430
And those are fine but they sound a little shaky.

1144
00:47:35,430 --> 00:47:39,910
They convey hesitation. They give the listener psychological room to doubt you.

1145
00:47:40,470 --> 00:47:43,670
But when you replace that vague belief with a declaration.

1146
00:47:43,670 --> 00:47:48,790
I am confident that I possess the skills required to deliver successful outcomes in this role.

1147
00:47:48,790 --> 00:47:52,870
It just changes the entire feeling. The listener immediately gets a cue of

1148
00:47:52,870 --> 00:47:53,750
self-assuredness.

1149
00:47:53,750 --> 00:47:57,350
And the interviewer who is probably taking notes quickly they just write down

1150
00:47:57,350 --> 00:47:59,630
Sounds confident, you're literally giving them the words

1151
00:47:59,630 --> 00:48:00,910
you want them to associate with you.

1152
00:48:00,910 --> 00:48:02,710
It's such an easy win,

1153
00:48:02,710 --> 00:48:05,430
but you have to have done the prep work to back it up.

1154
00:48:05,430 --> 00:48:07,250
Right, you can't just declare confidence

1155
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:09,550
if you haven't actually thought about why you're confident.

1156
00:48:09,550 --> 00:48:11,870
True verbal confidence is just a projection

1157
00:48:11,870 --> 00:48:13,870
of internal preparedness.

1158
00:48:13,870 --> 00:48:17,010
Okay, so now let's move to what might be

1159
00:48:17,010 --> 00:48:19,790
the single most valuable communication strategy

1160
00:48:19,790 --> 00:48:21,470
in this entire deep dive.

1161
00:48:21,470 --> 00:48:23,470
Mm, I think I know which one you mean.

1162
00:48:23,470 --> 00:48:26,870
Replacing the word but with the word and

1163
00:48:26,870 --> 00:48:29,650
in any kind of negotiation or collaboration.

1164
00:48:29,650 --> 00:48:33,980
The word but is a psychological erasure.

1165
00:48:33,980 --> 00:48:34,860
Explain that.

1166
00:48:34,860 --> 00:48:36,300
In professional conversations,

1167
00:48:36,300 --> 00:48:38,440
whatever you say before the word but

1168
00:48:38,440 --> 00:48:41,140
gets instantly canceled out or minimized

1169
00:48:41,140 --> 00:48:42,740
by the conflict that comes after it.

1170
00:48:42,740 --> 00:48:45,600
So if I say, I really admire our team's culture,

1171
00:48:45,600 --> 00:48:47,860
but the communication style needs to change.

1172
00:48:47,860 --> 00:48:49,340
The only thing your manager hears

1173
00:48:49,340 --> 00:48:51,740
is communication needs changing.

1174
00:48:51,740 --> 00:48:55,020
And it instantly creates an adversarial dynamic.

1175
00:48:55,020 --> 00:48:56,220
You versus them.

1176
00:48:56,220 --> 00:48:59,140
And this is so important in cross-cultural settings too.

1177
00:48:59,140 --> 00:49:01,880
In many cultures, using but can come across

1178
00:49:01,880 --> 00:49:04,460
as really aggressive or confrontational.

1179
00:49:04,460 --> 00:49:06,500
Whereas and maintains harmony

1180
00:49:06,500 --> 00:49:08,540
while still letting you state your truth.

1181
00:49:08,540 --> 00:49:11,000
You're linking two valid realities together

1182
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:12,780
without canceling either one out.

1183
00:49:12,780 --> 00:49:15,380
It creates a softer, more collaborative,

1184
00:49:15,380 --> 00:49:17,940
but still incredibly powerful statement.

1185
00:49:17,940 --> 00:49:20,540
Let's use the example of asking for a raise.

1186
00:49:20,540 --> 00:49:22,860
Instead of saying, I love working here,

1187
00:49:22,860 --> 00:49:24,660
but I can't stay at this salary.

1188
00:49:24,660 --> 00:49:26,180
Which sounds like an ultimatum.

1189
00:49:26,180 --> 00:49:28,480
It does. You create a partnership statement instead.

1190
00:49:28,480 --> 00:49:29,900
So what does that sound like?

1191
00:49:29,900 --> 00:49:34,100
I love working here, and I also know that the compensation rate I'm at is not sustainable

1192
00:49:34,100 --> 00:49:36,820
for me given my market value and my contributions.

1193
00:49:36,820 --> 00:49:42,620
Wow, that's a huge shift. You're acknowledging the good part, the relationship, and you're

1194
00:49:42,620 --> 00:49:46,420
introducing the difficult reality right alongside it.

1195
00:49:46,420 --> 00:49:51,140
It completely de-escalates the request. The manager doesn't immediately hear an

1196
00:49:51,140 --> 00:49:56,300
ultimatum, which is what but signals in a negotiation. You're stating your need

1197
00:49:56,300 --> 00:50:00,300
clearly, but you're framing it as a joint problem to solve together. Which is a

1198
00:50:00,300 --> 00:50:04,780
sign of supreme professional maturity and well, confidence. Exactly. Okay, so

1199
00:50:04,780 --> 00:50:09,620
moving from that strategic language into actual conflict, we have to address

1200
00:50:09,620 --> 00:50:14,060
the sheer frustration of dealing with passive aggression at work. Mm-hmm. It's

1201
00:50:14,060 --> 00:50:18,000
the kind of behavior that makes you feel like you're going crazy, especially

1202
00:50:18,000 --> 00:50:19,600
Especially when it's hidden in an email.

1203
00:50:19,600 --> 00:50:24,160
Like when someone writes, just circling back again on that request.

1204
00:50:24,160 --> 00:50:27,240
Again, it's so infuriating.

1205
00:50:27,240 --> 00:50:29,040
So what's the root cause here?

1206
00:50:29,040 --> 00:50:35,960
The analysis is that passive aggression happens because people lack the adult communication

1207
00:50:35,960 --> 00:50:39,320
skills to just say what they need directly.

1208
00:50:39,320 --> 00:50:40,840
They're deficient communicators.

1209
00:50:40,840 --> 00:50:42,660
It's often a learned behavior, right?

1210
00:50:42,660 --> 00:50:43,660
Very often.

1211
00:50:43,660 --> 00:50:47,440
It stems from childhood dynamics where maybe people in their family would hint at

1212
00:50:47,440 --> 00:50:50,800
what they wanted and then get resentful when nobody could read their minds.

1213
00:50:50,800 --> 00:50:52,720
Because they were too insecure to just ask.

1214
00:50:52,720 --> 00:50:57,120
Exactly. So the core strategy for you, the person on the receiving end,

1215
00:50:57,120 --> 00:50:58,800
has to be a mindset shift.

1216
00:50:58,800 --> 00:50:59,520
What's shift?

1217
00:50:59,520 --> 00:51:01,280
Don't take it personally. Get curious.

1218
00:51:01,280 --> 00:51:02,560
Assume it's not about you.

1219
00:51:02,560 --> 00:51:07,120
Right. Someone's being passive aggressive. They're acting from a place of their own insecurity,

1220
00:51:07,120 --> 00:51:11,520
not from a place of malice directed at you. And if you fight fire with fire

1221
00:51:11,520 --> 00:51:12,720
or you get defensive.

1222
00:51:12,720 --> 00:51:14,720
It just makes them backpedal even more.

1223
00:51:14,720 --> 00:51:16,080
They just shut down.

1224
00:51:16,080 --> 00:51:21,040
the goal is professional accountability. You have to calmly steer the conversation away from

1225
00:51:21,040 --> 00:51:27,520
ambiguous hinting and back toward direct adult communication. Which requires a lot of composure.

1226
00:51:27,520 --> 00:51:31,200
It does. And MacGough provides this very direct professional challenge,

1227
00:51:31,760 --> 00:51:35,600
but the delivery has to be perfect. Okay, what's the line? You say,

1228
00:51:35,600 --> 00:51:39,280
oh, did you mean that genuinely? Or were you trying to hint at something?

1229
00:51:39,280 --> 00:51:44,160
Whoa, okay, I have to pause here. Because my immediate thought, and I think a lot of listeners

1230
00:51:44,160 --> 00:51:49,760
thoughts is that that sounds incredibly risky. It sounds aggressive. It sounds really aggressive.

1231
00:51:49,760 --> 00:51:55,840
Yeah. Especially if the person is your boss. How on earth do you deliver that without sounding

1232
00:51:55,840 --> 00:52:00,320
sarcastic or like you're accusing them of something? That is the necessary challenge.

1233
00:52:00,320 --> 00:52:06,320
The tone is absolutely everything. It has to be delivered with genuine almost naive curiosity.

1234
00:52:07,040 --> 00:52:10,560
Like you truly didn't catch their meaning. So you're trying to help them clarify and

1235
00:52:10,560 --> 00:52:15,680
not catch them in a lie. Exactly. Neutral facial expression. Maybe lean in a little bit with

1236
00:52:15,680 --> 00:52:21,920
genuine confusion and then pause after you say it. The confidence here is in your ability to

1237
00:52:21,920 --> 00:52:27,840
stay completely calm while delivering a very direct challenge. Let's run through a scenario.

1238
00:52:27,840 --> 00:52:32,240
A co-worker slacks you late on a Friday. I finished my part of the report,

1239
00:52:32,240 --> 00:52:35,440
but I'm assuming someone else will review the data over the weekend.

1240
00:52:35,440 --> 00:52:41,520
classic PA. The confident response is not to fire back. It's to isolate the ambiguity.

1241
00:52:41,520 --> 00:52:46,080
So on Monday morning, you calmly reply. I saw your message about the data review.

1242
00:52:46,080 --> 00:52:50,800
Can you clarify who you intended to assign that to? Or are you asking if I can take that on?

1243
00:52:51,440 --> 00:52:56,400
You forced them to be direct. Okay, what about an email? An executive emails the team,

1244
00:52:56,400 --> 00:53:00,320
the final deck looks mostly fine, but I hope we can apply more diligence next time.

1245
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:05,280
That's criticism hidden behind a vague hope. So instead of ignoring it or getting upset,

1246
00:53:05,280 --> 00:53:09,440
you address the vagueness professionally. You reply, thank you for the feedback,

1247
00:53:09,440 --> 00:53:13,600
to make sure we apply the right diligence next time. Could you specify which sections

1248
00:53:13,600 --> 00:53:18,320
you felt were lacking? And what specific measure of diligence you'd like to see?

1249
00:53:19,760 --> 00:53:24,560
Again, you're forcing them to be specific. They either have to give you actionable feedback,

1250
00:53:24,560 --> 00:53:28,960
which is great, or admit they were just being vague. What are some other phrases?

1251
00:53:28,960 --> 00:53:34,080
There are other de-escalation tactics. Like a simple, should I read into that comment?

1252
00:53:34,080 --> 00:53:38,840
but delivered with a totally neutral non-sarcastic tone.

1253
00:53:38,840 --> 00:53:40,720
Or just, can you tell me more about that?

1254
00:53:40,720 --> 00:53:41,560
Exactly.

1255
00:53:41,560 --> 00:53:43,700
And you can also use negotiation tactics

1256
00:53:43,700 --> 00:53:45,640
like reflective questioning.

1257
00:53:45,640 --> 00:53:46,840
It sounds like you have a reason

1258
00:53:46,840 --> 00:53:49,560
for saying the deck only looks mostly fine.

1259
00:53:49,560 --> 00:53:51,600
When they hear you believe their comment has a reason,

1260
00:53:51,600 --> 00:53:53,720
they're more likely to explain the real issue.

1261
00:53:53,720 --> 00:53:54,960
Right, maybe they felt rushed

1262
00:53:54,960 --> 00:53:57,440
or they thought a key piece of data was missing.

1263
00:53:57,440 --> 00:53:59,100
Ultimately, the confidence comes

1264
00:53:59,100 --> 00:54:01,360
from your own emotional regulation.

1265
00:54:01,360 --> 00:54:04,140
You're treating their bad communication like data,

1266
00:54:04,140 --> 00:54:05,300
not a personal attack.

1267
00:54:05,300 --> 00:54:06,600
And you're calmly guiding them

1268
00:54:06,600 --> 00:54:08,500
toward being a better communicator,

1269
00:54:08,500 --> 00:54:10,660
which preserves your energy and your sanity.

1270
00:54:10,660 --> 00:54:11,500
Exactly.

1271
00:54:11,500 --> 00:54:13,800
So we've covered the foundation, the language.

1272
00:54:13,800 --> 00:54:15,660
